.TH CCHAN_CREATE_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cchan_create_mp \- create a two-way cchan with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

cchan *cchan_create_mp(size_t max_size,
                       ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a cchan made of two circular queues, one for each
direction: cchan_owner_to_workers and cchan_workers_to_owner. Both are
created with the same max_size, so the two directions have the same
capacity.

The calling thread becomes the owner of the channel, and its ID is
stored. Every later cchan_ call compares the ID of its own thread
with that one and routes itself from the result: the owner sends to
cchan_owner_to_workers and receives from cchan_workers_to_owner, and
every other thread does the opposite. That's why those calls take no
direction argument.

The two queues and the cchan struct itself are allocated through
mmgmt_procs. The channel keeps its own copy of the procedures and frees
itself through them, so the caller's ccol_memmgmt_procs_t doesn't have
to outlive this call. NULL selects the default malloc and free, which
is what the cchan_create() macro passes.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size     Maximum number of messages that each direction can hold.
             Because one circular queue holds each direction, the bound
             of ccircq_create_mp(3) applies: at
             least 1, no larger than ccol_max_elem_count, and no larger
             than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(cmessage_t).
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, a pointer to the new cchan, and *err_str is set to
NULL when err_str is not NULL.

On failure, NULL, and *err_str describes the reason when err_str is not
NULL. A failure has one of three causes:
mmgmt_procs is malformed because it sets some but not all of malloc,
calloc, realloc and free; an allocation failed; or the function could
not create one of the two circular queues below the channel, for any of
the reasons in ccircq_create_mp(3), such as a
max_size that is out of range. When the first queue is created and the
second one fails, the first one is freed before the call returns, so
no failure path leaves anything allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe. When you are done with the channel,
destroy it with cchan_destroy().

The owner thread, the one that called this function, must stay alive
for as long as the cchan is in use, because the channel routes a
message by comparing the caller's thread ID with the one it took here.
Once the owner exits, the OS can hand the same ID to a new, unrelated
thread, and the channel would then treat that thread as the owner too
without reporting anything.

fork(2) and a cchan can be combined in two supported ways: call
fork(2) before you create the cchan, or create it and then call
fork(2) followed at once by exec(3). A program that keeps running with
no exec(3) after the fork while a cchan from before the fork is
live is not supported. As a defense in depth beyond that rule, the
child never inherits a lock that another thread held, in the locked
state, at the instant of the fork. Neither queue below the channel owns
a thread of its own, so a cchan that a child inherits this way
stays fully usable.
.fi
